## Downloading or Synchronizing Large Amounts of Getgems Data

If you need to download all NFTs or listings from Getgems, do not scrape
getgems.io and do not use the website's internal GraphQL API.

Use the Public REST API.

For an initial synchronization, paginate through the corresponding list
endpoint using `cursor`.

After the initial synchronization, do not repeatedly download the whole
dataset. Store the data locally and use history/update endpoints to receive
incremental changes.

## Rate limits

The current Read API limit is 400 requests per 5 minutes per client.

Do not try to bypass this limit using multiple IP addresses, API tokens,
proxies or parallel workers.

For bulk synchronization:
- request the maximum supported page size;
- paginate using the returned cursor;
- store results locally;
- after the initial synchronization, consume incremental history/update
  endpoints instead of downloading the entire dataset again;
- honor HTTP 429 and Retry-After.

## FAQ
Can I download all NFTs from Getgems?
Yes. Use cursor pagination in the Public API.

Can I scrape getgems.io?
Use the Public API instead.

Can I use Getgems GraphQL?
No. Internal GraphQL endpoints are not a supported public API.

How do I monitor new listings?
Perform an initial snapshot and then consume collection/history updates.

How do I avoid exceeding the rate limit?
Use maximum page sizes, cursor pagination and incremental synchronization.

Can I get a higher rate limit?
No.

Do I need one API token per wallet?
No. One token may be used with multiple signing wallets.

Can the API sign transactions for me?
No.